        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#ownership" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Ownership Change, and Why It Matters</a>
          <a href="#the-range" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What Bowflex Actually Sells</a>
          <a href="#jrny" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. The JRNY Question</a>
          <a href="#hardware" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Hardware, Assessed Plainly</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Who Should Buy One</a>
          <a href="#existing-owners" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. If You Already Own One</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. What to Cross-Shop</a>
          <a href="#ownership-costs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. What It Costs to Own</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most Bowflex treadmill coverage was written before 2024 and describes a company that no
          longer exists in that form. The hardware is much the same; the company behind the
          warranty, the parts and the subscription is not, and that is the thing worth
          understanding before you spend money.
        </p>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Ownership Change, and Why It Matters</h2>
<p>
            Bowflex was a Nautilus brand for decades. In late 2023 Nautilus, Inc. renamed itself
            BowFlex Inc. after its best-known product line. In March 2024 that company filed for
            Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection, and its assets — the brand, the product lines, the
            intellectual property — were acquired by <strong>Johnson Health Tech</strong>, with the
            deal completing that spring.
          </p>
          <p>
            Johnson Health Tech is not a small buyer. It is a large Taiwanese manufacturer that
            already owns <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon
            Fitness</a>, <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix
            Fitness</a> and Vision Fitness, and which built the
            <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LiveStrong Fitness</a>
            range under licence years ago. It has been making treadmills at scale for a long time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For a buyer, that is reassuring rather than alarming.</strong> A brand emerging
            from bankruptcy is normally a reason for caution — orphaned hardware, no parts, nobody
            to honour a warranty. That is not what happened here. Bowflex was bought by an
            established manufacturer with its own factories, its own service network and a clear
            commercial reason to keep the brand alive.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For an existing owner, it is the answer to the question you were probably
            asking.</strong> Warranty administration and parts supply for pre-bankruptcy machines
            passed through the transition, and the practical route today runs through Johnson's
            support operation rather than the old Nautilus one. If you have been searching for
            Bowflex support and finding dead links, that is why — and the same cross-referencing
            logic our <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LiveStrong
            guide</a> describes increasingly applies here: approach it as a Johnson product.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The honest caveat.</strong> Chapter 11 does not carry every obligation across
            unchanged, and coverage on a machine bought before the filing is not automatically what
            the original paperwork said. If you own a pre-2024 Bowflex and the warranty matters to
            you, establish where you actually stand rather than assuming, and do it before something
            fails.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What Bowflex Actually Sells</h2>
<p>
            Two things are sold under this name and conflating them is the most common confusion in
            the category.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The treadmills</strong> are the T-series — the Treadmill 22, Treadmill 10 and
            Treadmill 9 in recent years, preceded by the BXT-series. These are conventional
            belt-over-deck home treadmills, differentiated mainly by screen size, and positioned in
            the mid-to-upper home market.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Max Trainer</strong> is not a treadmill at all. It is a hybrid
            elliptical-and-stepper machine, and it is what our existing
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex cardio guide</a> and the
            <a href="/bowflex-max-trainer-m7/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">M7</a> and
            <a href="/bowflex-max-trainer-m5/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">M5</a> reviews
            cover. A lot of people searching for a Bowflex treadmill land on Max Trainer content and
            come away confused, so it is worth stating plainly: different machine, different motion,
            different purpose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Within the treadmill range</strong>, the numbering broadly tracks the screen.
            The headline hardware across the recent T-series is fairly consistent: a deck sized for
            running rather than walking, incline to 15%, a top speed of 12 mph, and 300 lb capacity.
            The genuinely distinguishing feature is <strong>decline</strong> — the T-series offers
            negative grade as well as positive, which is unusual at this price and useful for anyone
            training for downhill running or working on eccentric loading. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline guide</a>
            covers what grade figures actually mean.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequence of the numbering is that choosing between T-series models is
            mostly a decision about how much you want to pay for a screen, not about how good the
            treadmill underneath is. That is a clearer decision than most ranges offer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="jrny" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The JRNY Question</h2>
<p>
            Bowflex machines are built around <strong>JRNY</strong>, the brand's subscription
            platform — adaptive workouts, streaming, on-screen coaching, the familiar arrangement.
            And the familiar question applies: what does the machine do without it?
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It runs.</strong> There is a manual mode; the belt moves, the incline and
            decline work, and basic metrics display. You are not left with a dead machine if you
            stop paying.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>But the console is built for the platform.</strong> On a machine whose main
            differentiator between models is the size of its screen, declining to subscribe means
            paying for a large screen and then using very little of it. That is a worse trade than
            it is on a brand like <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon</a>,
            where the console is deliberately plain and priced accordingly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>So the arithmetic works the way it does for every subscription machine.</strong>
            Add sixty months of JRNY to the hardware price before comparing against anything, exactly
            as our <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton guide</a>
            works through. If the subscription is genuinely going to be used, the total may still be
            competitive. If you already know you will cancel after the trial, you are shopping in the
            wrong part of the market, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">no-subscription
            roundup</a> is the relevant page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One genuine advantage worth noting:</strong> JRNY is a household-style
            subscription that covers Bowflex equipment broadly rather than a single machine. If
            there is already Bowflex kit in the house, the marginal cost of adding a treadmill is
            hardware only.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hardware" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Hardware, Assessed Plainly</h2>
<p>
            Strip off the screen and the subscription, because in eight years that is what you own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck length is adequate for running</strong> on the T-series, which is the
            specification that matters most and the one where a lot of similarly priced machines cut
            corners. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt
            size guide</a> explains why this matters more than almost anything else, and taller
            runners should still check the stride arithmetic in our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tall
            runner guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Decline is the standout feature</strong> and it is genuinely uncommon here.
            Downhill running loads the legs very differently from level or uphill work, and having it
            available at home is worth something to a trail or road racer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Capacity at 300 lbs is normal</strong> for the class rather than generous — see
            our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">capacity
            guide</a> for why rated and comfortable figures differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folding is assisted</strong> on the T-series, which our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-small-spaces/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">small
            spaces guide</a> identifies as the specification that decides whether a treadmill is
            actually folded rather than theoretically foldable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The screen is the risk.</strong> This is the structural weakness of every
            screen-led treadmill and it applies here. A frame and motor will run for a decade; a
            large integrated touchscreen tied to a platform is the component most likely to end the
            machine's useful life, and it cannot be replaced with a generic part. It is the same
            argument our <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton
            guide</a> makes, and the new ownership improves the odds on parts supply without
            removing the underlying exposure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Who Should Buy One</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy one if you want decline.</strong> It is the clearest reason to choose this
            range over its direct competitors, and at this price there are not many alternatives
            offering it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy one if you already own Bowflex equipment</strong> and are subscribed. The
            marginal cost is hardware only and the ecosystem argument is real.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy one if the current pricing reflects the transition.</strong> Brands moving
            through an ownership change are often discounted for reasons unrelated to the hardware,
            and a well-built treadmill from a manufacturer with a real service network is a
            reasonable thing to buy at a discount. Check our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">timing
            guide</a> before deciding today is the day.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy one if you will not subscribe.</strong> You are paying for a screen
            you will not use. Buy a
            <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.0 AT</a>
            or a <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F63</a> —
            plainer consoles, comparable or better frames, no platform — and note that the Horizon
            is now a corporate sibling built by the same parent.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy one if you only walk.</strong> The running deck, the decline and the
            screen are all irrelevant at 3 mph. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking picks</a>
            cost far less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Be careful buying a used pre-2024 machine.</strong> Not because the hardware is
            bad — it is not — but because warranty position and platform continuity on
            pre-bankruptcy units are exactly the things a private seller cannot tell you. Price it
            as an out-of-warranty machine and use our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">inspection
            checklist</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="existing-owners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. If You Already Own One</h2>
<p>
            This section is for the large number of people who bought a Bowflex or Nautilus
            treadmill before 2024 and have since found the support trail going cold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Approach it as a Johnson Health Tech product.</strong> That framing gets you to
            the right support operation faster than searching the old brand name, and it is the same
            move that unlocks parts for
            <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LiveStrong owners</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Establish your warranty position explicitly</strong> rather than assuming the
            original terms carried across intact. Do it while the machine is working.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Most of what fails is generic.</strong> Belts are sized by width and
            circumference and are widely available. Rollers, deck boards, drive belts, motor brushes
            and power switches are commodity parts. The brand-specific risk is concentrated almost
            entirely in the console and the controller board, which is the same picture on every
            screen-led machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Before condemning a dead console,</strong> rule out the cheap causes — safety key
            and magnet, the wiring loom at the folding pivot where the cable flexes thousands of
            times, the reed switch, the breaker. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">power fault guide</a>
            walks the sequence, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair guide</a> covers
            whether a given fix is worth paying for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep it lubricated.</strong> On any machine whose electronics are the
            irreplaceable part, friction is what finishes it — rising belt-to-deck drag makes the
            motor and controller work harder every month. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication
            guide</a> and <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval
            guide</a> cover it, and it is ten minutes every few months.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Cross-Shop</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Horizon, now a corporate sibling.</strong> Same parent company, plainer console,
            no platform, longer published frame and motor cover. The
            <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">7.0 AT</a> is the
            direct cross-shop for anyone who wants the hardware without the subscription, and the
            fact that both brands now come from the same factories makes this an unusually clean
            comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack, for the same proposition from a rival.</strong> Screen-led,
            platform-attached, decline available on some models. See our
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">brand guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole, for plain hardware.</strong>
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> builds heavy
            machines with long warranties and no account requirement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peloton, if the platform is the point.</strong> Our
            <a href="/peloton-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton guide</a> runs
            the five-year subscription arithmetic that applies equally here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A used commercial machine, if hardware is the point.</strong> At upper T-series
            money our <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used
            market analysis</a> shows what a gym refit buys, and
            <a href="/precor-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor</a> in particular
            reaches the secondhand market in quantity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What It Costs to Own</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Start with the subscription.</strong> Sixty months of JRNY alongside the hardware
            is the real five-year number, and on a screen-led machine it is not an optional extra —
            it is what the console was designed to deliver.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Maintenance is ordinary and cheap.</strong> Lubricant every few months, an
            eventual belt, occasional
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tracking</a>
            and <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tension</a>
            adjustment, and a vacuum of the
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor
            compartment</a> twice a year. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost
            guide</a> has the annual figures and they are small.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Electricity is minor</strong> — see our
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">usage guide</a>
            — and does not change any comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The real variable is the screen.</strong> Budget mentally for the possibility
            that a large integrated display is what eventually ends the machine, and weigh that
            against a plain-console alternative that will simply keep working. This is not a Bowflex
            criticism; it is true of every treadmill sold on the strength of its screen, and it is
            the single strongest argument for the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">plainer
            end of the market</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Resale is uncertain during a brand transition</strong>, which argues for buying
            to keep rather than buying to flip.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Bowflex now?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Johnson Health Tech. BowFlex Inc. — formerly Nautilus, Inc., renamed in late 2023 — filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in March 2024, and Johnson Health Tech acquired the brand and product lines, completing that spring. Johnson also owns Horizon Fitness, Matrix Fitness and Vision Fitness, and built the LiveStrong Fitness range under licence, so Bowflex is now under the same roof as several established treadmill lines.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Bowflex warranty still valid after the bankruptcy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Warranty administration and parts supply carried through the transition, and the practical route today runs through Johnson Health Tech's support operation rather than the old Nautilus one. But Chapter 11 does not carry every obligation across unchanged, so if you own a pre-2024 machine, establish your actual position explicitly — and do it while the treadmill is still working rather than after something fails.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a Bowflex treadmill work without a JRNY subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. There is a manual mode — the belt runs, incline and decline work, and basic metrics display without an account. But the console was built for the platform, and on a range where the main difference between models is screen size, declining to subscribe means paying for a large screen you barely use. If you know you will not subscribe, a plainer machine is better value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Bowflex Max Trainer a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The Max Trainer is a hybrid elliptical-and-stepper machine with a completely different motion, and it is a common source of confusion for people searching for Bowflex treadmills. The treadmills are the T-series — Treadmill 9, 10 and 22 in recent years, preceded by the BXT-series — and they are conventional belt-over-deck machines.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What makes Bowflex treadmills different from competitors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Decline. The T-series offers negative grade as well as incline, which is uncommon at this price and genuinely useful for anyone training for downhill running or working on eccentric loading. Otherwise the hardware is a competent mid-to-upper home specification, and the differences between models in the range are mostly about screen size rather than the treadmill underneath.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a used Bowflex treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The hardware is sound, but warranty position and platform continuity on pre-2024 units are exactly what a private seller cannot tell you. Price it as an out-of-warranty machine, inspect it properly, and remember that on a screen-led treadmill the display is the component with no generic replacement. Test every console function before paying.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Bowflex Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Two of these come from reading coverage written before the ownership change, which is most of what is online.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the bankruptcy means orphaned hardware</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A brand emerging from Chapter 11 usually means no parts and nobody to call. That is not what happened here — Bowflex was bought by Johnson Health Tech, an established manufacturer with its own factories and service network that also owns Horizon and Matrix. Approach support as a Johnson product rather than searching the old Nautilus trail, which mostly leads to dead links.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing the Max Trainer with the treadmills</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Max Trainer is a hybrid elliptical-stepper, not a treadmill, and a great deal of Bowflex review content is about it. If you are comparing cardio machines, make sure the review you are reading describes a belt and a deck. They are different products with different motions and different reasons to buy them.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a screen-led machine with no intention of subscribing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Within the T-series the main difference between models is screen size, so declining to subscribe means paying a premium for a display you will barely use. Add sixty months of JRNY to the hardware price before comparing against anything. If the total does not appeal, the plainer end of the market gives you a better frame for the same money.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring that the screen is the part that ends the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A frame and motor will run for a decade with lubrication; a large integrated touchscreen tied to a platform is the component most likely to fail terminally, and it has no generic replacement. That is true of every screen-led treadmill rather than this brand specifically, but it belongs in the decision — and it is the strongest argument for a plain console if you plan to keep the machine a long time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

